WEBVTT

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:19.840
data structures and algorithms, we are going to learn today some basic terminology regarding

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:25.480
data structures and the notations that we would be following in the rest of this course.

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:33.020
We will begin with just some very simple definitions, an algorithm which is an outline

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:39.520
of the steps that a program has to take or any computational procedure has to take.

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:46.860
A program on the other hand is an implementation of an algorithm, it could be in any programming

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:57.420
language. A data structure is the way we need to organize the data so that it can be used

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:03.400
effectively by the program. So you all are familiar with certain data structures, an

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:08.780
array or a list for instance. So this course is about you will be seeing a lot more data

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:13.100
structures in this course and you will see how to use them in various algorithms. So we

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:17.980
will take a particular problem try to solve that problem and in the process develop data

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:22.680
structures the best way of organizing the data associated with that problem.

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:30.680
So what is an algorithmic problem? An algorithmic problem is essentially that you have a certain

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:36.800
specification of an input as is given here and I use specify what the output should

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:42.900
be like and the specification of an input could be well here is one specification assorted

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:50.360
non-decreasing sequence of natural numbers of non-zero finite plane that is an input that

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:55.720
is a completely specified input. I have given two examples here of inputs which meet the

00:02:55.720 --> 00:03:00.960
specification. I have not given any output specification yet here. Now what is an

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:08.820
instance? These are two instances of the input. This is the specification for the input and

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:16.720
you can have any possible instance. You can take any sequence of sorted non-decreasing numbers

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:21.720
and that would form an input instance. So there are infinitely many input instances

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:30.960
possible here. So an algorithm is essentially describing the actions that one should take

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:37.660
on the input instance to get an output as is desired as is specified and again just as there

00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:42.900
can be infinitely many input instances there can be infinitely many algorithms for solving

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:53.610
a certain problem. Each one of you could do it in a slightly different way. So that

00:03:53.610 --> 00:03:57.030
brings us to the notion of a good algorithm. If there are so many different algorithms

00:03:57.030 --> 00:04:00.010
for solving a certain problem what is a good algorithm?

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:10.060
A good algorithm for us is an efficient algorithm. Anything that is efficient is good and what

00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:15.860
is efficient? Efficient is something which has small running time and takes less memory.

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:20.880
These will be the two measures of efficiency that we will be working with. There could

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:24.280
be other measures of efficiency but these are the only two we would be considering in

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:30.360
this course and most of our time we would be spending with running time really. Space

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:34.760
of course will also be analyzing the space but most of the time would be spent in worrying

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:40.430
about the running time of our algorithm and we would be interested in the efficiency of

00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:47.370
algorithms as a function of the input size. So clearly you can imagine that if I have

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:53.410
a small input and my algorithm running on that input will take or my program running

00:04:53.410 --> 00:04:57.670
on that input will take less amount of time. If the input becomes 10 times larger then

00:04:57.670 --> 00:05:02.210
the time taken by the program would also grow. May be it becomes 10 times, may be it becomes

00:05:02.210 --> 00:05:08.410
20 times, may be it becomes 100 times. I do not know. So it is this behavior of the increase

00:05:08.410 --> 00:05:13.670
in the running time with the increase in the size of the input that we would also

00:05:13.670 --> 00:05:19.610
be of interest to us. We will come to all of these in a short while as we go through

00:05:19.610 --> 00:05:27.170
these slides. Now how does one measure running time? I said efficiency, running

00:05:27.170 --> 00:05:32.010
time, very important first. So how does one measure the running time of an algorithm? So

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:38.370
one way would be to what I have put down here as an experimental study. You have a certain

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:43.610
algorithm, you implement that algorithm which means you write a program in a certain programming

00:05:43.610 --> 00:05:50.590
language. You run the program with varying data sets. So some smaller, some larger

00:05:50.590 --> 00:05:54.130
data sets, some would be of some kind, some would be of a different kind. So varying

00:05:54.130 --> 00:06:00.770
composition and then you clock the time that the program takes and clock does not mean that

00:06:00.770 --> 00:06:04.810
you sit down with a stopwatch, you would perhaps just use a system utility like let us say system

00:06:04.810 --> 00:06:11.450
current time may less to clock the time that the program takes and then from that you

00:06:11.450 --> 00:06:18.250
try and figure out how good your algorithm is. So that is what one would call an experimental

00:06:18.250 --> 00:06:27.920
study of the algorithm. Now this has certain limitations. So I have put them down, first

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:31.640
you have to implement the algorithm. To be able to determine how good your algorithm is

00:06:31.640 --> 00:06:38.940
you have to implement it. Now that is already a huge overhead. Considerable amount of time

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:43.180
has to be spent in doing this. Then your experiments can be done only on a limited

00:06:43.180 --> 00:06:47.520
set of inputs. After all I said the number of instances is infinitely large. You cannot

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:52.900
be running, you can only run your experiment on a small set of instances and that might

00:06:52.900 --> 00:06:57.540
not really be indicative of the time that your algorithm is taking for other inputs which

00:06:57.540 --> 00:07:05.150
you have not considered in your experiment. Further if you have two algorithms and you

00:07:05.150 --> 00:07:09.830
have to decide which is better, you have to use exactly the same platforms to do

00:07:09.830 --> 00:07:16.330
the comparison. By platform I mean both the hardware and the software environments because

00:07:16.330 --> 00:07:20.030
as you can imagine different machines would make a difference not just different machines.

00:07:20.630 --> 00:07:24.450
In fact even the users who are working on that system at that particular point would make

00:07:24.450 --> 00:07:29.890
a difference on the running time of your algorithm. So it becomes very messy if you have to do

00:07:29.890 --> 00:07:35.250
it this way. So what we are going to do as part of this course and in fact in this

00:07:35.250 --> 00:07:40.050
very first lecture is to develop a general methodology which will help us analyze the

00:07:40.050 --> 00:07:45.970
running time of algorithms. We are going to do it as follows. We are going to first

00:07:45.970 --> 00:07:52.710
develop a high level description of an algorithm, a way of describing an algorithm and we are

00:07:52.710 --> 00:07:59.650
going to use this description to figure out the running time and not implement it or any

00:07:59.650 --> 00:08:06.400
such thing. Our methodology would help us take into account all possible input instances

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:15.200
and it will allow us to evaluate the efficiency of the algorithm in a way that is independent

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:25.150
of the environment, independent of the platform that we are using. I said we will give a high

00:08:25.150 --> 00:08:28.410
level description of the algorithm. This very first point here we will give a high level.

00:08:28.510 --> 00:08:33.430
What is this? This brings me to what we will call pseudocode and this is how we are

00:08:33.430 --> 00:08:38.690
going to be specifying all our algorithms for the purposes of this course.

