00:12:20.150 --> 00:12:31.540
You will see more of this when we come across more pseudocode. So how do we analyze algorithms?

00:12:31.540 --> 00:12:39.080
So we identify what are primitive operations in our pseudocode. So what is a primitive operation?

00:12:39.260 --> 00:12:45.000
It is a low level operation. Examples, data movement. I do an assignment from one to another.

00:12:45.400 --> 00:12:50.980
I do a control statement which is a branch if then else, a subroutine call, a return

00:12:51.800 --> 00:12:57.400
or I do arithmetic operations or logical operations. These are all we will call them primitive

00:12:57.400 --> 00:13:02.000
operations and in my pseudocode I just inspect the pseudocode and count the number of primitive

00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:11.780
operations that are executed by the algorithm. So we need to take an example now. I will take

00:13:11.780 --> 00:13:16.860
an example of sorting. You all know what sorting is. The input is some sequence of

00:13:16.860 --> 00:13:29.090
numbers. Output is a permutation of the sequence which is in non-decreasing order. So if

00:13:29.090 --> 00:13:41.170
this were my input this should be my output. What are the requirements of the output?

00:13:41.170 --> 00:13:48.600
We said it should be in non-decreasing order and it should be a permutation of the input.

00:13:49.600 --> 00:13:54.860
Any set of numbers which are in non-decreasing order does not make an output. Your algorithm

00:13:54.860 --> 00:13:59.020
should sort the numbers that were given to it not just produce a sequence of numbers

00:13:59.020 --> 00:14:09.270
in increasing order. Clearly the running time will depend upon how many elements are

00:14:09.270 --> 00:14:16.310
there and quite often it depends upon how sorted these numbers were.

00:14:17.010 --> 00:14:22.590
If they were already almost sorted then perhaps your algorithm is not going to take a long time.

00:14:23.330 --> 00:14:28.690
Of course it also depends upon what particular algorithm you are using. So the running time

00:14:28.690 --> 00:14:35.370
would depend upon all of these factors. The first sorting technique we are going

00:14:35.370 --> 00:14:41.730
to use is one that you have used very often. Let us say when you are playing a game of

00:14:41.730 --> 00:14:51.410
cards. So what is the strategy you follow when you are picking up set of cards that have

00:14:51.410 --> 00:14:57.950
been dealt out to you? You like to keep them in a sorted order in your hand. So you start

00:14:57.950 --> 00:15:02.490
with your empty hand, you pick up the first card then you take the next card and you

00:15:02.490 --> 00:15:06.770
insert it at the appropriate place. Suppose you have some 5 cards in your hand already

00:15:06.770 --> 00:15:15.630
let us say 2, 7, 9, Jack and Queen then I get an 8 so I am going to put it between 7 and

00:15:15.630 --> 00:15:23.390
9. That is the right place it has to be in. So I am inserting it at the appropriate place

00:15:23.390 --> 00:15:29.880
and that is why this technique is called insertion sorting and I keep doing this till

00:15:29.880 --> 00:15:34.720
I have picked up all the cards and inserted them appropriately in the hand that I have.

00:15:38.540 --> 00:15:44.220
So what is the idea? This is the pseudo code for insertion sort and we will just run through

