be equal to j and when T sub j equals j, note what this quantity becomes. It is summation

00:25:32.300 --> 00:25:38.920
j, j going from 2 to n. You all know it is of the kind n squared and in that

00:25:38.920 --> 00:25:43.200
case the running time of this algorithm would be some constant times n squared plus some

00:25:43.200 --> 00:25:49.080
other constant times n minus some other constant. The behavior of this running time is more of

00:25:49.580 --> 00:25:53.800
more like n squared. We come to this point later when we are talking of a symptotic

00:25:53.800 --> 00:25:59.460
analysis but this is what I mean by f of n squared. It will behave more like n squared.

00:26:00.180 --> 00:26:06.700
Here on the other hand in the best case when Tj was 1, this sum here is just n or

00:26:06.700 --> 00:26:12.720
n minus 1 and in that case the total time is n times some constant plus n minus 1 times some

00:26:12.720 --> 00:26:23.170
constant minus some constant which is roughly n times some constant. So it is what we call

00:26:23.170 --> 00:26:30.250
the linear time algorithm. I come to these things again. On an average what would you

00:26:30.250 --> 00:26:33.510
expect? In the best case it is something like that you have to compare only against

00:26:33.510 --> 00:26:37.750
one element. In the worst case you have to compare about j elements. In the average

00:26:37.750 --> 00:26:42.950
case you would expect that you would compare against half of those elements. In the average

00:26:42.950 --> 00:26:49.410
case if you were to take it as you are comparing against j by 2, even then this quantity summation

00:26:49.410 --> 00:26:54.510
of j by 2, j going from 2 to n, what will this be? This will be roughly n squared by

00:26:54.510 --> 00:27:03.110
4 now. So its behavior is again like n squared and we come to these points in a minute.

00:27:03.110 --> 00:27:12.710
So this is what I mean by best, worst and average case. So I take the size of the input suppose

00:27:12.710 --> 00:27:19.770
I am interested in sorting n numbers and I look at all possible instances of these n numbers

00:27:21.370 --> 00:27:26.710
infinitely many. So again it is not clear how exactly one would do that. So what is

00:27:26.710 --> 00:27:32.690
worst case defined as? Worst case is defined as the maximum possible time that your algorithm

00:27:32.690 --> 00:27:39.990
would take for any instance of that size. So these are all instances of the same size.

00:27:42.650 --> 00:27:48.590
The best case would be the smallest time that your algorithm would take and the average would

00:27:48.590 --> 00:28:07.270
be the average of all of these infinitely many bars. So that was for inputs of one size

00:28:08.390 --> 00:28:15.510
n. That would give us the values of we can then therefore compute worst case, best case

00:28:15.510 --> 00:28:22.370
and average case. Now if I were to consider inputs of all sizes I can then create a plot

00:28:22.370 --> 00:28:27.130
of this kind. For each input size I can figure out, suppose I could figure out what the

00:28:27.130 --> 00:28:31.590
worst case, best case and average case were then I would get such monotonically increasing

00:28:31.590 --> 00:28:40.630
plots. Why is that? It is clear that as the size of the input increases the time taken with

00:28:40.630 --> 00:28:45.750
your algorithm will increase. It is not going to happen that your input size becomes larger

00:28:45.750 --> 00:28:55.040
and it takes lesser time. So which of these is of interest to us or which of this is

00:28:55.040 --> 00:29:05.700
easiest to work with? Worst case is the one that we will use most often and for

00:29:05.700 --> 00:29:12.900
why is that? First it provides an upper bound. It tells you how long your algorithm is going

00:29:12.900 --> 00:29:20.200
to take in the worst case. Many algorithms it occurs fairly often. Quite often it is

00:29:20.200 --> 00:29:26.780
the case that your worst case that for many instances the time taken by the algorithm

00:29:26.780 --> 00:29:33.820
is close to the worst case. So that average case essentially becomes as bad as the worst

00:29:33.820 --> 00:29:41.340
case. In fact for the previous example that we saw, average case was like n squared and

00:29:41.340 --> 00:29:45.100
worst case was also like n squared. Of course there were a difference in the constant but

00:29:45.100 --> 00:29:52.840
it was roughly the same. Now average case might be a very difficult quantity to compute

00:29:52.840 --> 00:29:55.680
because as I said average case if you have to compute you have to look at all possible

00:29:55.680 --> 00:30:02.340
instances and then take some kind of an average. Or you have to say my input instances

00:30:02.340 --> 00:30:07.920
are drawn from a certain distribution and so the expected time my algorithm will take

00:30:07.920 --> 00:30:11.920
if an input instance is drawn from this distribution is going to be similar. So it is typically

00:30:11.920 --> 00:30:20.220
a much harder quantity to work with and compute. So worst case is the measure of interest

00:30:20.220 --> 00:30:21.820
is what we will be working with.

00:30:27.340 --> 00:30:33.460
So asymptotic analysis is the kind of a thing that we have been doing so far when

00:30:33.460 --> 00:30:40.040
I said n squared and n and the goal of this is really to analyze the running time while

00:30:40.040 --> 00:30:50.840
getting rid of superficial details. We would like to say that an algorithm which has a running

00:30:50.840 --> 00:30:56.100
time of some constant times n squared is the same as an algorithm which has a running

00:30:56.100 --> 00:31:01.420
time of some other constant times n squared. Why? Because this constant is typically

00:31:01.420 --> 00:31:07.080
something which would be dependent upon the hardware that you are using. In the example

00:31:07.080 --> 00:31:12.860
that I showed you the C1, C2, C3 all of those times would depend upon the computer system

00:31:12.860 --> 00:31:18.160
that you are using. The hardware, the compiler, many many factors and so we would not like

00:31:18.160 --> 00:31:22.420
to distinguish or we are not interested or we are not bothered about distinguishing

00:31:22.420 --> 00:31:27.280
between such algorithms. Both of these algorithms one which has a running time of 3 n squared

00:31:27.280 --> 00:31:33.620
perhaps or another with the running time of n squared have a quadratic behavior as in when

00:31:33.620 --> 00:31:42.920
the input size doubles both of the algorithm their running time increases 4 fold and that

00:31:42.920 --> 00:31:44.400
is the thing that is of interest to us.

00:31:45.980 --> 00:31:50.820
We are interested in capturing how the running time of the algorithm increases with the size

00:31:50.820 --> 00:31:59.080
of the input in the limit. This is the crucial point here and that is what asymptotic

00:31:59.080 --> 00:32:03.940
analysis is all about. That is what the asymptotic here is. In the limit how does the running time

00:32:03.940 --> 00:32:07.960
of this algorithm increase with increase in input size.

00:32:12.530 --> 00:32:17.650
That brings us to something that some of you might have seen before the Bego notation.

00:32:19.690 --> 00:32:26.830
So if I have functions f of n and g of n, f of n and g of n, n here is typically representing

00:32:26.830 --> 00:32:35.770
the input size. f of n is measuring the time that your algorithm is taking. So f of n and

00:32:35.770 --> 00:32:43.190
g of n are both non-negative functions not just non-negative they are also non-decreasing

00:32:43.910 --> 00:32:51.570
because as input sizes increases the value of the running time, the time taken by your

00:32:51.570 --> 00:32:52.730
algorithm would also increase.

00:32:54.990 --> 00:33:03.280
So these are both non-decreasing functions of n and we say that f of n is Big O of g of n.

00:33:04.220 --> 00:33:12.440
If there exists constant c and n0 such that f of n is less than c times g of n for n larger

00:33:12.440 --> 00:33:16.780
than n0. So what does all of this mean? So I have drawn two functions here. The function

00:33:16.780 --> 00:33:22.020
in red is f of n and g of n is some other function. This function in green is some constant

00:33:22.020 --> 00:33:30.560
n times g of n. Now as you can see beyond this point n0, c times g of n is always larger than

00:33:30.560 --> 00:33:39.020
f of n. So suppose this is the way it continues even beyond this point. Then we would say that

00:33:39.020 --> 00:33:51.680
f of n is Big O of g of n or f of n is order g of n. So a few examples would clarify this

00:33:51.680 --> 00:33:52.880
and we come to that.

00:33:56.340 --> 00:34:10.740
My function f of n here is 2n plus 6 and g of n is n. It is the case, if you look at these

00:34:10.740 --> 00:34:19.670
two functions 2n plus 6 is always larger than n. So which is why this function 2n plus 6

00:34:19.670 --> 00:34:24.410
is always above n. Now you might be wondering why 2n plus 6 is a non-linear function

00:34:24.410 --> 00:34:33.180
here. That is because the scale here is an exponential scale. So it is 2 to the 0 to the

00:34:33.180 --> 00:34:42.760
1 to the 2 and so on and similarly on this axis. So this is n and this blue line here

00:34:43.250 --> 00:34:56.110
is 2n and this is 4n. Now as you can see beyond this point f of n is less than 4 times n.

00:34:57.130 --> 00:35:06.150
So what are our constant c and n0? The constant c is 4 and n0 would be this point of crossing

00:35:07.210 --> 00:35:21.140
beyond which 4n becomes larger than 2n plus 6. What is this point of crossing? At what

00:35:21.140 --> 00:35:33.780
point does 4n become larger than 2n plus 6? 3. So your n0 becomes 3 and then we say

00:35:33.780 --> 00:35:46.450
that f of n which is 2n plus 6 is big O of g of n. Not big O of 4n but big O of n. So f of

00:35:46.450 --> 00:35:58.440
n here is big O of n. So 2n plus 6 is big O of n. Another example, the function in red

00:35:58.440 --> 00:36:04.540
is g of n which is n and any constant time g of n, so it is the same scale as in the

00:36:04.540 --> 00:36:09.940
previous line. Any constant time g of n will just be the same straight line displaced by

00:36:10.580 --> 00:36:17.400
suitable amount. So this will be 4, this will be 4 times n, this will be whatever depends

00:36:17.400 --> 00:36:24.240
upon what the intercept here is. So it will just be like this but your n squared would

00:36:24.240 --> 00:36:35.630
be a line like this. So there is no constant c such that n square is less than c of n.

00:36:35.790 --> 00:36:41.430
Can you find out a constant c such that n square is less than c of n for n more than n0? No. Any

00:36:41.430 --> 00:36:48.010
constant that you choose I can pick a larger n. I can pick an n such that this is violated

00:36:49.690 --> 00:37:01.590
and so it is not the case that n square is big O of n. So how does one figure out these

00:37:01.590 --> 00:37:09.010
things? So there is a very simple rule. Suppose this is my function 50n log n. I just drop

00:37:09.010 --> 00:37:17.710
all constants and lower order terms. So forget the constant 50 here and I get n log n. So this

00:37:17.710 --> 00:37:26.790
is big O of n log n. This function 50n log n is big O of n log n. 7n minus 3 is I drop

00:37:26.790 --> 00:37:32.230
the constant and lower order terms I get 7n minus 3 is big O of n. I have some complicated

00:37:32.230 --> 00:37:38.130
function like 8n square log n plus 5n square plus n. I just drop all lower order terms.

00:37:38.130 --> 00:37:44.170
This is the fastest growing term because this has n square as well as log n in it. So I just

00:37:44.170 --> 00:37:49.510
drop the n square and n term and from here I drop my constant and I get n square log n.

00:37:50.190 --> 00:37:58.290
So this function is big O of n square log n. There is a constant c such that this quantity

00:37:59.490 --> 00:38:07.210
this large sum here is less than c times n square log n for n larger than some n0.

00:38:07.210 --> 00:38:15.190
In the limit this quantity will be less than some constant times this quantity and you can

00:38:15.190 --> 00:38:19.930
figure out what the value of c should be and what the value of n0 should be for that to

00:38:19.930 --> 00:38:20.170
happen.

00:38:22.070 --> 00:38:32.990
This is a common error. So this function 50n log n is also big O of n to the 5. Why?

00:38:32.990 --> 00:38:41.530
Because this quantity in fact is less than or equal to 50 times n to the 5 always for all

00:38:41.530 --> 00:38:50.710
n and that is just a constant. So this is big O of n to the 5. But when we use the big

00:38:50.710 --> 00:38:55.730
O notation we try and provide as strong a bound as possible. So instead of saying while

00:38:55.730 --> 00:39:06.570
this statement is true we will rather call this as big O of n log n. You will see more

00:39:06.570 --> 00:39:17.630
of this in subsequent slides. So how are we going to use the big O notation? So we are going

00:39:17.630 --> 00:39:26.200
to express the number of primitive operations that are executed during a run of the program

00:39:26.200 --> 00:39:30.900
as a function of the input set. We are going to use the big O notation for that. Now if

00:39:30.900 --> 00:39:39.540
I have an algorithm for which the number of primitive operations is big O of n and

00:39:39.540 --> 00:39:42.900
some other algorithm for which the number of primitive operations is big O of n square

00:39:42.900 --> 00:39:47.960
then clearly the first algorithm is better than the second. Why? Because as the input

00:39:47.960 --> 00:39:53.080
size grows, if the input size doubles then the running time of this algorithm is only

00:39:53.080 --> 00:39:59.840
going to double while the running time of this algorithm will increase fourfold. Similarly

00:39:59.840 --> 00:40:04.640
our algorithm which has a running time of big O of log n is better than one which has

00:40:04.640 --> 00:40:08.720
a running time of big O of n. And we have a hierarchy of functions of this kind log

00:40:08.720 --> 00:40:13.640
n n n square and an algorithm with the running time of big O of log n is better than one with

00:40:13.640 --> 00:40:17.480
the big O of n with better than one with the big O of running time of big O of n square

00:40:18.120 --> 00:40:22.660
running time of big O of n cube which is better than an algorithm with the running time of

00:40:22.660 --> 00:40:28.660
big O of 2 to the n. So there is a word of caution here. You might have an algorithm

00:40:28.660 --> 00:40:35.560
whose running time is a million times n because you are doing a whole lot of maybe

00:40:35.560 --> 00:40:40.140
some other operations. I cannot see how you would create such an algorithm but you might

00:40:40.140 --> 00:40:46.460
have an algorithm of this running time. So this is big O of n because this is less than or

00:40:46.460 --> 00:40:50.300
equal to some constant times n and you might have some other algorithm with the running

00:40:50.300 --> 00:40:56.300
time of 2 n square. So from what I have said before you would say that this algorithm

00:40:56.300 --> 00:41:02.840
is better than this. The one with the linear running time of big O of n running time is

00:41:02.840 --> 00:41:09.240
better than this. It is true but in the limit and the limit here is achieved very late when

00:41:09.240 --> 00:41:15.500
n is really really large. For small instances this 2 n square might actually take less time

00:41:15.500 --> 00:41:22.100
than your 1 million times n. So you have to be careful about the constants also.

00:41:22.100 --> 00:41:35.060
So we will do some examples of asymptotic analysis. So I have a pseudocode written here and what

00:41:35.060 --> 00:41:44.020
I am doing here is I have an array of numbers, n numbers sitting in an array called x and

00:41:44.020 --> 00:41:56.730
I have to output an array a in which each element in which the element ai is the average of the

00:41:56.730 --> 00:42:07.050
numbers x0 through xi. This is what I have to do. So what is one way of doing it? So I

00:42:07.930 --> 00:42:17.570
basically have a for loop in which I compute each element of the array a. So to compute

00:42:17.570 --> 00:42:27.290
a10 what should I do? I just have to sum up x0 through x10 which I am doing here. So what

00:42:27.290 --> 00:42:35.050
is this doing? When I am computing a10 i is taking the value 10 and I am running the

00:42:35.050 --> 00:42:45.630
index j from 0 to 10. I am summing up the value of x0 plus x1 up to x10 in this accumulator

00:42:45.630 --> 00:42:53.070
a and then I am eventually dividing the value of this accumulator with 11 because it is x0

00:42:53.070 --> 00:43:02.790
through x10 and that gives me the number that I should have in a10 and I am going to repeat

00:43:02.790 --> 00:43:15.610
this for 11, 12, 13, 14 all the elements essentially. Is this a good algorithm? It is not a good

00:43:16.130 --> 00:43:24.530
algorithm. This is one step. How many times is this step executed? It is executed i times

00:43:25.390 --> 00:43:31.690
and what are the values that i takes? Initially i takes a value 0 to 1, 2, 3 all the way up

00:43:31.690 --> 00:43:40.980
to n minus 1 and this entire thing is done n times. So what does that give you? That

00:43:40.980 --> 00:43:46.780
gives you total running time of roughly n square. This one step is getting executed n square

00:43:46.780 --> 00:43:53.300
times and this is the dominant thing. Of course how many times is this step getting executed?

00:43:54.820 --> 00:43:59.720
This step is getting executed n times and so is this step which is getting executed n times

00:43:59.720 --> 00:44:04.860
but this step is getting executed roughly n square times, some constant times n square

00:44:04.860 --> 00:44:10.740
times and so running time of your algorithm is bigger of n square. It is a very simple

00:44:10.740 --> 00:44:16.220
problem but you can have a better solution here and perhaps all of you can see what

00:44:16.220 --> 00:44:22.480
the better solution should be. What is the better solution? We will have a variable s in

00:44:22.480 --> 00:44:49.360
which we would keep accumulating the x i's. Initially s is just 0 and then when I am computing

00:44:49.720 --> 00:44:55.140
because I used that at the last step. That is the problem that is happening here. See every

00:44:55.140 --> 00:45:00.460
time we are computing x, so first we are computing x0 plus x1, then we are computing x0 plus x1

00:45:00.460 --> 00:45:05.000
plus x2, then we are computing x0 plus x1 plus x2 plus x3. We are kind of repeating

00:45:05.000 --> 00:45:10.140
computations over and over again. Why should we do that? We will have just the single

00:45:10.140 --> 00:45:18.780
variable which will keep track of the prefixes, the sum of the prefixes so far. So s at

00:45:18.780 --> 00:45:24.720
this point when I am in the ith run of this loop, s at that point already has the sum x0

00:45:24.720 --> 00:45:31.200
through x i minus 1 and then I sum x i in it and to compute the ith element I just need

00:45:31.200 --> 00:45:37.780
to divide this sum by i plus 1. I keep this accumulator round with me. Now when I finish

00:45:37.780 --> 00:45:47.180
the ith run, ith iteration of this loop I have in s x0 through x i, the sum x0

00:45:47.180 --> 00:45:54.620
through x i. I can reuse it for the next step. So how much time does this take now? In each

00:45:54.620 --> 00:46:02.300
run of this loop I am just doing two steps, two primitive operations. So that makes it

00:46:02.300 --> 00:46:09.020
order n time because this loop is executed n times.

00:46:16.090 --> 00:46:21.930
So I have been using this freely, linear and quadratic but here is a slide which just

00:46:21.930 --> 00:46:27.750
tells you the other terms that I might be using. So linear if an algorithm has a running

00:46:27.750 --> 00:46:33.210
time, I say an asymptotic running time of order n, then we call it a linear algorithm. If it

00:46:33.210 --> 00:46:37.710
has an asymptotic running time which is n squared, we call it quadratic. Logarithmic

00:46:37.710 --> 00:46:44.630
if it is log n, polynomial if it has n to the k for some constant k, k is a constant

00:46:44.630 --> 00:46:50.250
here. And an algorithm is called exponential if it has a running time of a to the n

00:46:50.710 --> 00:46:59.870
where a is some number more than 1. So till now I have only introduced to you the big o

00:46:59.870 --> 00:47:12.040
notation. We also have the big omega notation and the big theta notation. The big omega

00:47:12.040 --> 00:47:19.780
notation provides a lower bound. A function f of n is omega of g of n. So this is f of

00:47:19.780 --> 00:47:27.000
n, this is constant times g of n. If constant times g of n is always less than f of n,

00:47:28.120 --> 00:47:33.840
earlier this was more than f of n, less than f of n in the limit. So beyond a certain n0

00:47:35.120 --> 00:47:45.280
as this picture illustrates. So f of n is more than c times g of n beyond this point n0 and

00:47:45.280 --> 00:47:53.960
that case we will say that f of n is omega of g of n. The theta notation f of n is theta

00:47:53.960 --> 00:48:00.240
of g of n if there exist constant c1 and c2 such that f of n is sandwiched between

00:48:00.240 --> 00:48:06.980
c1 times g of n and c2 times g of n. That is beyond a certain point f of n lies between

00:48:07.480 --> 00:48:12.120
one constant times g of n and another constant times g of n. So then we say that f of n is

00:48:12.120 --> 00:48:25.960
theta of g of n. That is f of n grows like g of n in the limit. Another way of thinking

00:48:25.960 --> 00:48:31.040
of it is f of n is theta of g of n if f of n is big o of g of n and it is also

00:48:31.040 --> 00:48:41.000
omega of g of n. This part tells you that f of n is big o of g of n and this part tells

00:48:41.000 --> 00:48:45.940
you that f of n is omega of g of n and so we have that f of n is theta of g of n.

00:48:48.150 --> 00:48:53.510
There are two more related asymptotic notations. One is called the little o notation and the

00:48:53.510 --> 00:48:58.130
other is called the little omega notation. They are the non tight analogues of big

00:48:58.130 --> 00:49:07.470
o and sorry they are the non tight analogues of big o and big omega and it is best to understand

00:49:07.470 --> 00:49:13.890
this through the analogy with real numbers. So when I say that f of n is big o of g of

00:49:13.890 --> 00:49:19.650
n, I am really saying that the function f is in some sense less than or equal to g.

00:49:22.700 --> 00:49:28.020
That is in fact what we used in the definition. f of n is less than c times g of n. So f

00:49:28.020 --> 00:49:33.760
the analogy with real numbers is when a number is less than or equal to another number. Omega

00:49:33.760 --> 00:49:39.760
is for greater than or equal to theta is equal to of course these are functions while these

00:49:39.760 --> 00:49:45.260
are real numbers. So if these are real numbers you can talk of equality but you cannot talk

00:49:45.260 --> 00:49:50.420
of equality of functions unless they are exactly equal. So this is the corresponding notion

00:49:50.940 --> 00:50:01.100
for that. Little o now corresponds to strictly less and little omega corresponds to strictly

00:50:01.100 --> 00:50:05.640
more. So we are not going to be using these. In fact we will be spending most of our time

00:50:05.640 --> 00:50:10.000
with just big o. So that is the part you should be very clear with.

00:50:10.540 --> 00:50:12.920
What do we specifically mean that it is strictly?

00:50:13.800 --> 00:50:21.080
For that I will just maybe look at the slide. So the formal definition for little

00:50:21.080 --> 00:50:29.100
o is that for every constant c there should exist some n0 such that f of n is less than

00:50:29.100 --> 00:50:37.020
c times g of n for n more than n0. How is it different from big o? There it said there

00:50:37.020 --> 00:50:42.440
exists a c and an n0 such that this is true. Here we want to say for every c there should

00:50:42.440 --> 00:50:54.550
exist an n0 for which this is true. So this is just one slide that I put up to

00:50:54.550 --> 00:50:59.610
